<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaroslavl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaroslavl is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotorosl">Kotorosl</a>. Founded in the early 11th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, it was the seat of its own princes from 1218 until Moscow absorbed it in 1463. In the 17th century wealthy merchants built its many richly painted churches, and its historic centre is a World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaroslavl.jpg" alt="Yaroslavl" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allmyexesliveintexas22 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslavl_Russia_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its princes 'gathered the Russian lands' by purchase, inheritance, marriage, diplomacy and war, absorbing Nizhny Novgorod in 1392, <b>Yaroslavl</b> in 1463, Rostov in 1474, the Novgorod Republic in 1478, Tver in 1485, Vyatka in 1489, Pskov in 1510 and Ryazan around 1521.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The alliance joined the Pereyaslavl, <b>Yaroslavl</b>, Yuryev, Nizhny Novgorod and Murom principalities.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yaroslavl</b> was founded in the early 11th century by Yaroslav the Wise at the meeting of the Volga and the Kotorosl; a legend has him killing a bear there.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-yaroslavl">Principality of Yaroslavl</a></li></ul></section>
